    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Standing in Steve Mann’s lab at the University of Toronto may be the closest thing to stepping inside his brain. Circuit boards, old computers, and other "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "electronics",
            "ref": "v-electronics"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " fill every corner. Boxes of "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "manuals",
            "zh": "说明书"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " and tools are "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "piled",
            "zh": "堆放"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " so high that they nearly touch the ceiling."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Mann, a professor at the university, keeps it this way "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "on purpose",
            "ref": "v-purpose"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". “Since childhood, I’ve been interested in how things work,” he says. “I’ve always filled my space with all kinds of cool, techy things.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Indeed, Mann, now 63, has always had a sense of wonder about technology. When he was four, his grandfather taught him to "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "weld",
            "zh": "焊接"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". The boy noticed that the small window of the protective helmet almost completely blocked his view. Mann kept this “problem” in mind for years. Finally, in 1993, he came up with a solution: HDR. The technology combines images with different "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "exposures",
            "zh": "曝光时间"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " to create a clearer picture. It’s now used everywhere, from digital cameras to smartphones."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "As a teenager, Mann volunteered at a repair shop. Using the spare parts in the shop, he built his first wearable computer, the WearComp. It weighed about 36 kilograms. Mann admits it was "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "inconvenient",
            "ref": "v-inconvenient"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " to use, but it worked."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Bit by bit, Mann made it smaller, lighter, and more useful. With the development of HDR, it finally became the EyeTap. It’s likely the world’s first pair of smart glasses. Mann was so proud that he wore them almost 24/7. But many people thought it was strange. Some even crossed the street to avoid Mann."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Beyond the HDR and the EyeTap, Mann also created a smartwatch videophone, 16 years before the Apple Watch hit the market. “A lot of what I do seems ahead of its time,” he says. “Most of my ideas come to me in dreams. Somehow, they walk into the future.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p7",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Mann still has plenty to say about where technology is headed. Hopefully, we’ll all start listening."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
